Say what frame the surface is born with, and withdraw the claim in patch 0082.

PATCH 0082 STATES THAT THE WAYLAND SURFACE IS 47 PIXELS SHORTER THAN THE WINDOW. IT IS NOT. The
surface is faithful, and this trace is what showed it.

-[NSWindow _createPlatformWindowOnMainThread] now prints the frame and the content rect at the
moment the platform window is created, gated on CIDER_TRACE_TOOLBAR. For MoneyMoney Preferences:

    CIDER_WINCREATE class=MMWindow frame={697.0,-9.0 406.0x331.0} content={406.0x253.0}
    cider-wayland-window create=ok number=4 size=406x331 at=697,-9 level=0 style=0x3

The frame IS 406x331 when the surface is born. The compositor gets exactly what AppKit asked for.

The 406x378 I compared it against in 0082 was measured EARLIER, during toolbar setup, when the
content was still 300. The application resized its own window in between, which is what a
preferences window with panes does. 331 is 253 content plus 56 toolbar plus 22 title bar, and the
on screen interior measures about 309, which is 253 plus 56. Every number agrees.

THAT IS THE SECOND TIME ON THIS TASK I HAVE COMPARED TWO VALUES THAT WERE NEVER TRUE AT THE SAME
MOMENT. The first was an assigned origin against a surviving one (0081, withdrawn in 0082). This
was a frame from setup against a frame at show time. The rule both times: a comparison needs both
sides sampled at the same instant, and a trace that prints only one side invites exactly this.

WHAT IS LEFT OF #255, now narrow. The window is exactly as AppKit intends, so the overlap is INSIDE
it: the pane view draws above the content view, into the toolbar strip. The overflow is 47 points,
which is 300 minus 253, the difference between the size the nib gives the pane and the size of the
content view it is placed in.

Also ruled out by reading, so nobody repeats it: frameRectForContentRect: and
contentRectForFrameRect: are symmetric. Both adjust by the main menu height and by the toolbar view
height, so they do invert each other and a setContentSize: does not lose the toolbar. And
constrainFrameRect:toScreen: only moves the origin, never the size.

Off by default.

Task #255.

